#4: Syconycteris australis, Dobsonia viridis, Cynopterus sphinx, Epomophorus wahlbergi and Rousettus leschenaultii AKA the Common Blossom Bat, Greenish Naked-Backed Fruit Bat, Greater Short-Nosed Fruit Bat, Wahlberg's Epauletted Fruit Bat, and Leschenault's Rousette
#5: Fruit bat phylogeny is relevant in the context of recent disease outbreaks and global health concerns. Fruit Bats are well known for being "reservoirs" of several viruses including SARS-CoV-2 (coronavirus responsible for COVID-19 Pandemic), Ebola Virus, and more. With the COVID-19 Pandemic having been a severe disruption to public health, with many lives having been lost, a phylogenetic tree can help us trace disease origins to see which species are most closely related to known carriers, to narrow down the source. It can also help us to understand virus evolution (example: COVID-19 has had several major variants, including Alpha, Delta, and Omicron) and guide experts towards solutions and prevention of spread.
